        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Change online to offline in the Service Worker section of the first PWA tutorial]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/43404</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/43404"/>
        <updated>2026-03-11T02:10:44.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/615227?u=6e9d97e970d30390a8e94b9177402c3abe1f18f3&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">I might be wrong, but from the context it seems that this should read "if the user is offline" instead of online. I searched around the issue tracker, and reviewed the code comments on <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="1755926454" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/27314" data-hovercard-type="pull_request" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/pull/27314/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/27314">#27314</a>, but there were no mention of it.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">In my view it makes more sense to specify what should happen in case the user is offline. It probably doesn't matter a lot in the case of this tutorial's example because the Service Worker will return 404 for any file outside the cache independent of the network being available or not.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Related issues and pull requests</h3>
<p dir="auto">I couldn't find any open issues or PRs related to this tiny potential issue.</p>
]]></content>
        <author>
            <name>agarie</name>
            <email>agarie@noreply.github.com</email>
            <uri>https://github.com/agarie</uri>
        </author>
        <published>2026-03-11T01:59:40.000Z</published>
    </entry>
